WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.410
Over the past two weeks,

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.830
I've been spotlighting
various black voices

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:08.540
to learn as much as I can
during these insane times.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.490
My guest tonight is a
very talented comedian

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.220
and actor who has carved
his own unique path

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.610
through the entertainment industry.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.850
He's also one of the most
loving fathers I know.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:23.130
His new show Nice One
will be premiering later

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.520
this summer on Quibi, please welcome my

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.380
really good friend Ron Funches.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:28.740
Ron, good to see you.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.447
Hey Coco, how you doing?

00:00:30.447 --> 00:00:32.130
How dare you call me Coco.

00:00:33.112 --> 00:00:35.452
How dare, you know, disrespect me?

00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:38.030
(laughing)

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.940
You know, we have so much to talk about.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.170
Obviously, the last two weeks
has been so many things,

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:49.170
it's been traumatic and
upsetting and at times violent

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.560
and there's, I mean, I think
what good is coming out

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.300
of it is that there's
been a lot of discussion

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.420
and we've been trying to
do that here on the show.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.740
One of the things I wanted to talk

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.740
to you about is that when,
when you saw what happened

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.880
to George Floyd, I'm sure
this brought up a lot

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.880
of feelings about your
own son, your son who has,

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.120
you've worried about,
you've told me in the past

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.000
that you about your son and
about your son's autism,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.750
and that this has been a specific concern

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.360
of yours for his safety out in the world.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:36.193
Is that true?

00:01:36.193 --> 00:01:37.130
Yeah, absolutely.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.590
It's been a concern of
mine for many years.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.300
It's one of my most motivating things

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.300
in my life just that because
of him being mixed race,

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.890
meaning half black and half
white and having autism

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.750
and not being, you know,

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:56.280
he's just doesn't follow
directions as easily.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.630
He doesn't respond to
directions as quickly

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:01.860
as some people would like
and I was always concerned

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.910
that that if he was in an altercation

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:04.910
with a police officer

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.713
that that could mean the end of his life.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.140
So it was something that
was deeply concerned

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.400
to the point where, you know,

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.480
I didn't necessarily like him
out of my sight that much,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.340
I didn't and a lot of
my motivation was trying

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.000
to insulate that with money.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.900
Being like, Okay, if we can surround him

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.490
in a good neighborhood,
if we can surround him

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:32.270
with a nanny that's there,
even though he's 17 years old,

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:34.820
we have a full time nanny
just to make sure that someone

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:39.160
is with him and it's not
because he's not independent,

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.140
he's very independent it's a lot to do

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.040
with my fear about what could happen

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.610
with misunderstandings with others,

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.800
especially people like the
police or people with power.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.800
Yeah, and you know I was talking yesterday

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:57.230
to the mayor of Saint Paul,

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:02.220
Minnesota and we were
talking about this concept

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:06.380
that maybe in some situations you don't,

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:09.680
it shouldn't be automatic
that the police are called,

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.560
that it might be some
sort of health workers

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.180
that are called for some situations

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:16.960
so that response is a little more nuanced

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.960
and that's something you've
thought about with your son,

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:23.290
which is, you know, you
don't necessarily want

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:27.170
the police showing up if
you're having an issue

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.140
with your son.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.590
No, and that that really says a lot right

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.600
there that if you are in a bad situation,

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.560
if you're, if things are
feeling out of control,

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:38.830
like you don't want the
police because the fear

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.320
is that they will escalate the situation.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.130
Their job is usually to
just try to get the peace

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.640
and order as quick as possible

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.540
and if that means removing a situation,

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.690
if that means they have to shoot someone,

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:51.590
it seems like that is a quicker option

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:55.360
to them then trying to figure
out what the problem is,

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.470
trying to be empathetic
to what the problem is.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.750
It's something I dealt with
when my son was was younger,

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:03.200
when he was five, six, seven years old

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.380
and he would be overwhelmed
by different stimulus,

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.440
he would have these big tantrums

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.890
and it would involve
people calling the police

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:16.860
to my house and I was lucky
that it never escalated

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:18.320
but it did end up with like, you know,

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.160
no one ever came over and say, hey,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.580
what can we do to help?

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:22.470
What was the issue?

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:23.303
What's was going on?

00:04:23.303 --> 00:04:25.210
Instead, they would come
over, they would tell us

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.360
to shut it down and shut him up

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.760
and then they would call Child Services

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.160
and then we'd have to deal with that.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.270
It was always like, you're a bad parent

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.890
or you don't know what
you're doing as opposed to,

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.060
you need help and I think that's something

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.640
where health services and people trained

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.430
in that can be much more
helpful especially dealing

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:47.430
with anyone with any type of
mental diversity or disability,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.700
you don't want someone who's not familiar

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.230
with that coming into the situation.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:54.860
I believe that your son Malcolm

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.653
is named after Malcolm X, is that right?

00:04:56.653 --> 00:04:58.150
Yes, that is correct.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:01.800
It's interesting because when
I Look at the actual footage

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.780
of Malcolm X, he's the most one

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.810
of the most charismatic person

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:11.810
that people I've ever seen
captured in documentary footage.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:15.130
He's got that same thing that Muhammad Ali

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:19.480
had where he can just, he's
such a powerful presence and ...

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:20.890
He can rapture you.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:24.210
Yeah, exactly when did you, I mean,

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.450
obviously if you named your son Malcolm

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:27.820
must have been an important figure to you,

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:29.540
is this your whole life?

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:30.920
It started probably around when I was 11,

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:33.380
12 years old while I
was still getting ready

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:35.240
to leave Chicago.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:36.960
One of the best things that my school

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.990
ever did for me was take me
to go see the autobiography

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.580
of Malcolm X when it was
starring Denzel Washington

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.290
and that kind of started
my infatuation with him

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.350
and then I read his autobiography

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:50.460
and then I started to read more speeches

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.100
that he had done and then it became clear

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:57.100
to me that like this was a guy
who had become marginalized

00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:00.900
by history as just like
just black separatists

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.220
that all his stuff was
about, like, you know,

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:06.370
separating black people from
white people and that was then,

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:07.300
that was not the truth.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:10.420
It was all about black love and pride

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:14.050
and a lot of it was just about power

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:19.050
and oppression and
unionizing and healthcare.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.810
He talks about a lot of
things about unionizing

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:24.820
and healthcare that are
still needed to this day,

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:28.920
that we don't deal with
and I think it's so funny

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:33.390
that people go back and
they glorify rightfully

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:37.840
so Martin Luther King, but they
also whitewash his history.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:42.350
He was not as, he was more
militant than people think

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:45.380
and Malcolm X was less
militant than people think.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.760
They both just talked about,
they were like two sides

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.210
of a coin that were headed
to the same direction

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:53.050
and I think, you know,

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:55.990
now they they invoke
Martin Luther King's name

00:06:55.990 --> 00:07:00.350
to kind of pacify us, to
kind of say your supposed

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:02.780
to just march and speech and you know

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.890
that Martin Luther did
and if you don't do that,

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.120
you're disrespecting Martin
Luther King and it's just like,

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.240
I don't wanna hear that from when he

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.230
was on the FBI most wanted list,

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:13.980
when he was murdered by our government,

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:18.040
when those are things that
now they celebrate him

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.310
when they did this damnedest
to discredit him just,

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:26.110
to say that he was a
womanizer and to kill him,

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.220
and then now, you know,

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:30.100
they're trying to tell another story.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:33.557
Right, one of the things that, you know,

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:35.740
I don't think I was unaware

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:40.740
of this but what I'm hearing
again and again from friends

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:46.523
of mine is, who are black is that they've,

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:51.970
they say that they
experienced racism sometimes,

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:54.790
but it's almost at a frequency

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:56.880
that their white friends don't pick up

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.650
on it but black people pick up on it.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:02.460
And that's something that
you're quite familiar with,

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.420
you'll be in a situation
where something might happen

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:07.460
and you'll feel Wait a
minute, that didn't feel right

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:12.320
to me but your white friends
who you think are pretty woke,

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:14.560
don't hear it.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:15.720
Is that true?

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:20.720
Yeah, it is true, it's
seems to be just operation.

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:24.903
How do I put it?

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:26.713
Do you think that you have to prove

00:08:26.713 --> 00:08:29.250
it to them, but if they
don't see it firsthand,

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.260
if it's not someone
calling you the N word,

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.930
if it's not someone
throwing you to the ground,

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.310
I think that's what made
the George Floyd situation

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:40.060
so unavoidable that you
could look at it directly

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:41.950
and you see the smugness, you see the lack

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:45.840
of empathy you see the
fact that he has his knee

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:47.420
on the back of a person's neck,

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:51.700
which is not needed for any
situation, so you can avoid it.

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.650
But a lot of the things you can avoid,

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:56.040
a lot of the things that people
would come to me and like,

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:58.560
maybe you misheard or
maybe you just picked up

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.390
the wrong tone or maybe you had a bad day

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:04.410
and I've been lucky that
like my best friend,

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.610
my friend Gabe who features for me,

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:10.460
he's been more open to it
and it's probably because he

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:13.220
had a black stepdad for a
while, he had black members

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:16.800
in his family and I remember
recently we were traveling,

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.560
and we just stopped in
this airport for coffee

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.790
and then the guy was
giving out coffee to people

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.750
and my friend was before me
and he talks to my friend,

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:28.130
he's like hey good morning,
how's everything going?

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:28.963
How's your day?

00:09:28.963 --> 00:09:29.796
Where are you headed to?

00:09:29.796 --> 00:09:33.210
And he gets to me and he
doesn't say a word to me.

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:36.670
He just, I just order
he gives me my coffee

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.860
and he shuts completely down
and then the next person

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:42.060
that comes by is the
white lady and he's back

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:45.360
to the same and so then
I was like, Oh Okay,

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.210
I get it and then I was
fortunate at that time

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:50.500
that my friend was there
to look over and go,

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:53.069
that guy's a he's a freak racist

00:09:53.069 --> 00:09:55.520
and sometimes that's what you need,

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:57.550
it's not necessarily
that that ruined my day

00:09:57.550 --> 00:09:59.650
or I cared that much about that coffee.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:02.390
I have learn to this situation of like,

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:03.860
I'm just passing through,

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:06.450
if that's how he's living
he's having a horrible day

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:08.760
and he's racist then
that's where he belongs,

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.410
just serving coffee out in the middle

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:12.480
of nowhere I'm passing through.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.530
But what sometimes that we
need is just validation,

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:19.090
just knowing that, hey,
even if you didn't see it,

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:22.550
even if you don't wanna
believe it, it happens

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:24.820
and you gotta take people's word for it.

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:26.220
I think that's the thing
that I've been seeing

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:29.870
the most is that, like, the
African American community

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:33.200
is the only group that has to prove

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:35.340
that they didn't deserve to die, you know,

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:38.720
that we have to explain
why, what did he do?

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:39.700
Why you get something?

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:44.190
Well, George Floyd had a
record, he did this 2007,

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:46.330
he did this 2009, it's like,

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:48.950
there's no other race
there's no other group

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:51.200
that you'd have to explain
why you don't deserve

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:52.260
to be murdered.

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:53.770
Yeah, yeah.

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:58.280
You and I went on the tour a year ago,

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:01.610
a little over a year
ago, and we spent a lot

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:04.430
of time together and so was there anything

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:08.680
that happened while we
were on that tour together

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:12.220
that that made you feel at all strange

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:15.690
or feel marginalized while
we were on the road together

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:18.064
or on different tours?

00:11:18.064 --> 00:11:20.630
I have done some different
tours definitely,

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:22.720
I wouldn't say there was
any event that happened

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:23.553
on our tour.

00:11:23.553 --> 00:11:26.650
I would say the only
thing that I was aware

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:30.850
of is that I had this mindset
that if I strayed too far

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:33.220
from the group that some
people might be like,

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:34.053
what are you doing?

00:11:34.053 --> 00:11:36.990
Why are you like, why are
you trying to get into this?

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.410
Like, are you like trying to rob

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:40.690
them or are you trying like,

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:42.620
you don't belong here basically.

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:44.489
Wait, why did you feel this is?

00:11:44.489 --> 00:11:47.633
It's a feeling that I carried,

00:11:47.633 --> 00:11:50.550
that I should not get
too far from the group,

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:54.046
that I should had to show
them that I was with you.

00:11:54.046 --> 00:11:54.879
Right.

00:11:54.879 --> 00:11:56.520
It is because if I felt
that if I was alone,

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:58.860
they'd go why are you in this fancy hotel?

00:11:58.860 --> 00:11:59.980
Why are you here?

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:01.320
What are you doing here?

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:02.863
And that happens to me more on like,

00:12:02.863 --> 00:12:05.440
because I love to travel,
I love to vacation

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:08.250
and that's happened to me
several times on vacation

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:12.240
or several times in my
stores where I would,

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:14.290
I used to do this thing that
if they treat me poorly,

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:16.430
I'd buy more stuff so that I proved

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:19.531
to them you were wrong
and now I was like no,

00:12:19.531 --> 00:12:21.168
I'm just not gonna make
sure you just don't get

00:12:21.168 --> 00:12:22.744
that commission, you know.

00:12:22.744 --> 00:12:24.910
(laughing)

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.928
So what you're saying is in order to prove

00:12:26.928 --> 00:12:29.280
that you were there with good intent,

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.520
you would like buy a canoe even
if you didn't want a canoe?

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.120
(laughing)

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:35.083
Yes.

00:12:35.083 --> 00:12:36.212
I think its the canoe.

00:12:36.212 --> 00:12:38.462
(laughing)

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:41.030
That's the strangest response

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:42.374
to feeling marginalized.

00:12:42.374 --> 00:12:43.530
(laughing)

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:45.130
It's not you see it a lot of time,

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:46.290
it's called peacocking.

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:47.860
Is it something that is happens

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:49.919
in the black community
where you see a lot of like,

00:12:49.919 --> 00:12:53.080
fancy cars, fancy clothes, because this

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:57.380
is a showcase of like, hey,
you shouldn't treat me poorly,

00:12:57.380 --> 00:12:59.870
instead of it being
like I'm a human so you

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:01.380
shouldn't treat me poorly.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:04.420
You know, that's one of the
biggest lessons I learned

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:06.810
as a young black man is that I thought,

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:08.963
for some strange dumb
reason I thought like,

00:13:08.963 --> 00:13:12.050
if I get a little bit of money
then they'll stop treating me

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:14.170
like this and then I learned

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:15.479
that that wasn't the case.

00:13:15.479 --> 00:13:17.000
(laughing)

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.380
So you'll be on it, I mean,
you've done very well,

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:21.920
you'll be on a vacation, you'll
be staying in a nice place

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:25.040
and you'll sometimes sense
that people are wondering,

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:26.010
how is he here?

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:27.330
What's he doing here?

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.130
Is he a guest of this hotel?

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:32.000
I've had it happen,
I've had a bellhop tried

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:34.810
to stop me from entering
my own hotel room.

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:36.750
Because I went outside for a jog

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:39.010
and I come back in my
shorts, and I'm sweaty

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:41.240
and he's just like, hey,
what are you doing here?

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:42.420
What are you doing here?

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:43.970
And then I have headphones

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:45.890
on so I'm not even paying attention,

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:48.680
so I just go walking by
and he starts following me

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:50.790
and yelling at me like, Who are you?

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:51.730
What are you doing here?

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:55.600
So I just turn around and
I show him my key card

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:56.818
and then I flip them off.

00:13:56.818 --> 00:13:59.734
(laughing)

00:13:59.734 --> 00:14:01.040
That was into that.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:02.140
Right, right.

00:14:02.140 --> 00:14:05.466
I'm glad I'm glad you
had the correct response,

00:14:05.466 --> 00:14:07.063
I mean you were able to do that.

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:12.183
There's this belief some
people have that racism is,

00:14:14.270 --> 00:14:17.140
that it's a generational aspect to racism

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:22.140
that it's worse with older
generations but as we move on

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:25.390
and people get younger and
newer generations come along,

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:26.593
it's getting better.

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:30.560
That is not always been
your experience, right?

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:33.300
No, not always, I mean I do believe

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.030
that there is always progress

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:38.240
and it's I think the more that
we become culturally mixed

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:41.200
the more that these kids
have grown up off of hip hop

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:43.560
and and seeing black people
in positions of power

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:46.030
and representation, the better it gets.

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:49.610
But no, I know plenty you turn
on a game of Call of Duty,

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:51.500
you turn on any video game

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:54.350
and it's not your grandpa
calling me the N word you know,

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:57.217
it's a 12 year old kid,
it's a 13 year old kid

00:14:57.217 --> 00:15:00.950
and you see it at these
Trump camp rallies,

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:02.777
they're not 80 year old people.

00:15:02.777 --> 00:15:03.610
Right, right.

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:06.310
They're 20 year old white
men who have found a way

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:08.800
to make themselves feel disenfranchised,

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:10.810
and make themselves feel like the victim

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:13.530
and use that national that white pride

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:15.280
and that racism to fuel themselves.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:18.630
So to me, that's one of
the biggest disservice we

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:22.826
do as a community is going,
well we just need to wait 10,

00:15:22.826 --> 00:15:24.751
20 years for our grandparents to die.

00:15:24.751 --> 00:15:25.584
Right, right.

00:15:25.584 --> 00:15:26.570
And it's like, well, we've
been waiting 100 years

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:29.680
for that and so those
grandparents, teach the kids

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:33.930
and teach the grandkids and you
got to look within yourself,

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:35.620
it's not just about your parents.

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:40.620
You have a very unique
comedic voice and style

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:45.440
and it's something I've always
really admired about you

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:47.790
and yet at the same time,

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:49.570
I'm imagining it's been a struggle

00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:52.950
for you to not get typecast

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:55.180
because of the color of your skin.

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:57.750
Yeah, absolutely, absolutely.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:01.080
I knew that from very first
when I went to Montreal

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:03.700
when I was a new face
gentleman, Brandon Johnson,

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:05.560
who was a great African American comedian,

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:08.080
and an actor came up
to me and he was like,

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:11.790
I saw your set, he's like,
you're weird and you're black,

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:15.210
your role is gonna be different
and longer and strange

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:18.210
but keep being you, don't
ever change for these people

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:23.210
and I've always kept that
and it's been a source

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:27.030
of anger and frustration
for me that my comedy

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:30.300
is built off of love,
is built off of empathy,

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:32.600
is built out of optimism, it's built off

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:35.700
of making the best of a bad situation

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:38.160
and a lot of times the
roles that are offered

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:40.230
to me are that of a gang member,

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.810
or that of a guy fresh out of jail,

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:46.430
or that of a guy where I have to change

00:16:46.430 --> 00:16:49.479
the way I speak, to speak more urban to,

00:16:49.479 --> 00:16:52.560
to fit into a role that
they want me to fit

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:55.880
into and I just don't
feel that that's fair,

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:59.223
especially in this time period, you know,

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:02.410
we still representation i think

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:03.680
is one of the biggest things,

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:05.270
laws are gonna be very important

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:08.730
just not police brutality
obviously is the step,

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:12.570
is a great step but
representation and being willing

00:17:12.570 --> 00:17:16.100
to show different facets
of the black community

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:19.110
is something that we still
struggle with every day,

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:22.690
every pilot season, I get
just more and more sitcoms

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:25.020
where either it's a struggle role

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:27.820
of a black guy lifting
themselves out of poverty,

00:17:27.820 --> 00:17:31.360
or it's clearly a white
roll that they're trying

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:32.820
to shoehorn some diversity

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:36.490
into and there's just
multiple black stories

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:38.360
and multiple types of way that people

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:40.293
can be viewed in the community
and that's something I try

00:17:40.293 --> 00:17:44.350
to write towards, and try to
create because I don't like

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:46.483
the roles that I'm offered, basically.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:54.870
I know that you've been
on my show many times,

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:56.940
always been hilarious.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:58.720
You did stand up on my
show once and I know

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:01.510
there was a joke you wanted to to do

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:03.460
or that you were planning to do,

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:06.460
and the joke involved
you using the N word,

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:11.460
and I don't think I was aware
of this at the time but one

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:13.270
of our producers said, well, you can't say

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:17.170
that word on Turner Broadcasting.

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:20.520
So we were in the position of being white

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.380
and telling you you can't use the N word,

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:24.583
which was just sort of a weird situation.

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:28.650
Was that something that
bothered you when that happened?

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:30.210
Of course it bothered me a little bit

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:33.410
but it wasn't something
that I didn't expect,

00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:35.690
you know, I was like let me attempt to do

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:37.680
this joke because I love it and again,

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:40.470
I think it shows my style
of someone who looks

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:43.800
at for the positive in
every negative situation.

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:47.100
But I didn't expect you guys
to let me say N word on TV,

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:51.530
so I wasn't mad about it but
I you know, when you look

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:55.550
at it you go like this
is just a weirder form

00:18:55.550 --> 00:19:00.190
of oppression where there's
a guilt about this word that,

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:02.250
you know, that we've taken
as the black community

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:04.810
to as a source of empowerment for us

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:06.020
and then they go like okay,

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:07.580
well now that you guys don't hate

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:08.910
it as much you can't say it.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:11.160
(laughing)

00:19:12.570 --> 00:19:15.143
Can I hear the joke or wait?
Can we hear the joke now?

00:19:15.143 --> 00:19:16.000
Yeah, I can tell you I mean,

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:18.700
I don't remember is 100%
because it was years ago,

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:20.350
but I remember the event.

00:19:20.350 --> 00:19:23.630
It was just about the time
I was gonna go get my son,

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:25.880
Papa Murphy's Take 'N' Bake pizza,

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:27.710
where he was walking across my apartment

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:29.720
across this two lane, big street

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:32.540
that we had and as I was
growing across the street,

00:19:32.540 --> 00:19:36.610
I hear this guy yell, hey,
use the crosswalk N word

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:38.940
and it pissed me off
because I was like man,

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:40.590
like there's so much racism in the world,

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:42.260
everybody's full of hatred I'm just trying

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:43.740
to get my son a pizza.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:47.590
But then I thought, Oh
yeah, he's in a car,

00:19:47.590 --> 00:19:50.900
maybe I just didn't hear the
rest of the conversation.

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:52.850
He and what he was trying to say was,

00:19:52.850 --> 00:19:57.018
use the crosswalk nigga I'm
worried about your own safety.

00:19:57.018 --> 00:19:57.968
You know like okay.

00:19:57.968 --> 00:20:00.218
(laughing)

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:05.772
That's a really good joke.

00:20:05.772 --> 00:20:07.920
(laughing)

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:10.660
You're always able to see the
silver lining I guess, huh?

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:11.866
Yes, I try.

00:20:11.866 --> 00:20:14.490
(laughing)

00:20:14.490 --> 00:20:17.293
He's worried about my safety.

00:20:17.293 --> 00:20:19.100
(laughing)

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:21.853
You know, we've hung out
a lot, we spend a lot

00:20:21.853 --> 00:20:26.410
of time together, we've been
in vans, we've been on planes,

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:28.303
we've been backstage.

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:31.440
I'm trying to figure out as best I

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:35.830
can, how I can, you know, how I can learn

00:20:35.830 --> 00:20:37.550
and how I can grow and evolve

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:40.630
and so if you have advice
for me, I'll take it.

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:44.080
Sure, I was thinking about this last night

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:49.080
cause I think a lot of people, alright,

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:51.000
are in your position a lot of white people

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.090
are at a position where they're,

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:54.561
this is new to them.

00:20:54.561 --> 00:20:56.890
(laughing)

00:20:56.890 --> 00:21:01.890
Which is hilarious to us
but I appreciate you trying

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:04.430
to look in, but I think
a lot of it is like,

00:21:04.430 --> 00:21:09.244
look don't be so egotistical
it's not about you,

00:21:09.244 --> 00:21:13.100
it's about us, it's about the things

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:14.200
that we're going through and the fact

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:16.790
that you can be listening
and open to that is great.

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:19.270
But I thought I did a
little bit more thinking

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:21.210
and I thought the best
thing I could tell you

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:26.210
is just be an active ally, be if you see,

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:30.790
you're in a privileged position
that sometimes white people

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:33.160
will reveal their racism
to you because you're white

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:36.630
and they don't think that
you might have an issue

00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:38.840
with it or they might
feel more comfortable

00:21:38.840 --> 00:21:42.270
with revealing their true
selves to you as opposed

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:45.570
to me you know, and I think
if you are in that position

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:47.540
and you see that to never let that go.

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:49.420
To never always check people

00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:52.816
if they are in that position
and you see them acting out

00:21:52.816 --> 00:21:55.390
alone, you know, or
treating someone poorly

00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:57.860
to never let them know that that is okay.

00:21:57.860 --> 00:22:00.590
That is the thing that is the best thing

00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:01.423
that you can do.

00:22:01.423 --> 00:22:05.530
Okay, okay, I have had the
experience over the years

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:08.380
of African Americans,Black people

00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:09.900
whenever I see them in their familiar

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:11.750
with my show they always
say the same thing,

00:22:11.750 --> 00:22:12.880
you're crazy.

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:13.713
(laughing)

00:22:13.713 --> 00:22:16.350
Yeah, Black people love you

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:19.840
for as white and lily white
is you are you are loved

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:20.690
in the Black community.

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:23.700
I think it's because you
are so awkwardly tall

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:26.481
and look like an outcast yourself.

00:22:26.481 --> 00:22:27.750
(laughing)

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:28.920
I always say the same thing,

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:32.888
they start laughing and they
say you're crazy, you're crazy.

00:22:32.888 --> 00:22:33.721
Yes.

00:22:33.721 --> 00:22:35.130
That always and actually, it's a nice way

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:36.523
to start the conversation.

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:40.713
You know which works out?

00:22:41.890 --> 00:22:46.230
Well, I really appreciate
you coming on the show

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:48.760
and having this conversation with me

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:52.913
and I want to apologize
again, when we were on tour,

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:57.740
singing songs towards you
with my acoustic guitar

00:22:57.740 --> 00:22:59.736
when I think I knew it drove you crazy.

00:22:59.736 --> 00:23:01.986
(laughing)

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:06.202
Literally love to be like

00:23:06.202 --> 00:23:07.410
(laughs)

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:10.980
it's me at my absolute
whitest which is so white

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:13.100
and I would see you and I'd
be like, I'm gonna do it again

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:16.045
and you were very vocal about it.

00:23:16.045 --> 00:23:17.321
(laughs)

00:23:17.321 --> 00:23:19.266
And you considered it a
unique form of torture.

00:23:19.266 --> 00:23:21.550
(laughing)

00:23:21.550 --> 00:23:25.370
Well, yeah about this what I
love about our relationship

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:28.160
is that I can say, I can tell you that,

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:29.590
I can say it.

00:23:29.590 --> 00:23:30.423
That's what?

00:23:30.423 --> 00:23:31.340
You didn't just tell me that,

00:23:31.340 --> 00:23:35.080
you then went out in
front of, I don't know,

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:38.050
3000 people in a giant venue

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:41.090
and you told them the most
horrible thing just happened

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:44.974
to me when I was backstage
playing this guitar.

00:23:44.974 --> 00:23:47.500
(laughing)

00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:50.440
You made fun of me brutally
in front of all these people

00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:52.991
and I loved it, it was
absolutely hilarious

00:23:52.991 --> 00:23:54.016
and I had to comment.

00:23:54.016 --> 00:23:55.640
It needed to be, you know,
I figured if it happened

00:23:55.640 --> 00:23:58.890
to me that much it must happen
to your staff all the time.

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:00.893
So I needed to stand up for them.

00:24:00.893 --> 00:24:03.143
(laughing)

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:07.200
You did the right thing,
you did the right thing.

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:09.660
Well, thank you so much
for doing this, I love you,

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:12.587
I can't wait till we get
to hang out in person

00:24:12.587 --> 00:24:16.360
and thank you so much
for doing this today.

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:17.860
You're a good man, thank you.

00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:20.060
I love you too, I
appreciate you doing this.

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:22.080
This is a great thing that you're doing

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:26.060
and I think I like that
you're looking into yourself,

00:24:26.060 --> 00:24:28.470
but you've always,
you've done a great job,

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:31.610
you've hired a lot of
diversity and the fact

00:24:31.610 --> 00:24:35.920
that you had Deon Cole on your
show when I was a young man,

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:38.440
it meant a lot to me as
a young Black comedian,

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:40.713
because it was one of the
few guys where I was like,

00:24:40.713 --> 00:24:42.790
this is not he's not doing a stereotype,

00:24:42.790 --> 00:24:44.080
he's not doing a stick.

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:46.640
He's a weird Black dude doing himself

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:48.290
and you gave him a platform to show

00:24:48.290 --> 00:24:52.270
that and then in turn that empowered me.

00:24:52.270 --> 00:24:55.210
Yeah, he's such a funny guy
and you're such a funny guy

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:57.260
and well, I'll see you soon,

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:59.155
I promise you'll never hear me sing again.

00:24:59.155 --> 00:25:00.320
(laughs)

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:01.449
Please.

00:25:01.449 --> 00:25:03.240
(laughing)

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:06.210
See, if all I do is, if I can grow

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:07.630
that much I can grow more.

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:10.070
All right, thank you very much Ron

00:25:10.070 --> 00:25:11.370
and I'll talk to you soon.

